{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01SDhfxa9W3C8dbLN5DBiGiL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There's an `extra_turns` mechanism. For \"skip turns\", I need to think about how to implement it.\n\nThe spec says \"target opponent skips their next X turns\". In MTG, when a player \"skips\" a turn, they simply don't take that turn — the turn is skipped. This is different from \"extra turns\" (which adds turns).\n\nLooking at the engine's `advance_phase`:\n```python\nif self.extra_turns:\n    self.active_player_index = self.extra_turns.pop(0)\nelse:\n    self.active_player_index = self._normal_next_index\n    self._normal_next_index = 1 - self._normal_next_index\n```\n\nFor \"skip turns\", the simplest approach is to add a `skip_turns` attribute on the player and modify `advance_phase` to skip that player when it would be their turn. In a 2-player game where the active player cycles between 0 and 1, if player 1 should skip their next turn, I need to check whether it's their turn and decrement the skip counter instead of making them active.\n\nAdding skip turn logic... The double flip actually preserves the alternation correctly—when a skip happens, both indices flip back to their pre-skip state, so the next turn resumes the normal rotation as if the skipped player never took their turn.\n\nFor handling randomness, I'm adding a fallback approach where Ral's effect checks if the game has an `rng` attribute and uses it, otherwise creates a default Random instance—this keeps the card logic self-contained without modifying the engine, and tests can inject a seeded RNG for deterministic results.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how to implement Surveil 2, which lets a player look at the top two cards of their library and choose which ones go to the graveyard versus back on top in any order.\n\nFor the −1 ability that targets multiple players for discard, I need to figure out how the engine stores targets for loyalty abilities—looking at existing planeswalker implementations, they seem to use either `_resolve_targets` or `self.chosen_targets`, and I need to verify which one the engine actually populates when a loyalty ability is activated.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":363,"cache_read_input_tokens":151166,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":363},"output_tokens":34,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8ad560dd-f0de-48bf-a5ec-5012d23eae4b","uuid":"a37a32de-1fb2-406d-9b48-1aff5d35c3ab","request_id":"req_011CbwC6Uw8m8EXAW4ZRmvRa"}
